But hmm — the existing ladder ALSO defines `var parkedHeadVf = parkedHeadSha(...)` further down; I'd move it up and delete the later duplicate. Also `headChangedNoVerdictVf` becomes dead (condition: parkedHeadVf known && differs && verdict ≠ failure — the first two imply the new rung fired). Remove that rung? It sits between the gh-952 rung and machineUnparkVf. With the new structure, when the else-branch runs, parkedHeadVf is either unknown/'' or equal to uHead → headChangedNoVerdictVf always false → dead code. Remove it and fold its rationale into the new rung's comment. That keeps the ladder honest.

The gh-952 rung (in the actorVf===null branch) — also dead (same condition + more). Remove it? Its comment is long. Since gh-994's rung strictly dominates, yes remove to avoid dead code... but "minimum targeted fix". Removing dead code that tests pin requires test updates anyway. I'll remove both dead rungs and update the pinned tests to the gh-994 contract. Actually wait — careful: gh-952 rung fires when `actorVf === null || parkedAtVf === null`. New rung fires regardless of those probes → dominates. Yes, dead. Remove.

Hmm, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the new rung should fire even when parkedHeadVf is a valid recorded sha but the PR is a GUEST and head unchanged... no — rung only fires when heads differ. Fine.

One subtlety — ticket AC3: "detector compares full SHAs (not prefixes, not branch names)". parkedHeadSha extracts 40-hex; uHead comes from prStatus/github_get_pr — full sha. Good.

Race I3: single removeLabel per tick, no re-add (SET gated by unparkedThisPass). Good.

Now — the audit log format from the ticket: `park-reset: <pr> head <old>→<new>`. I'll log:
